.TH LESS 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
less \- view text one screenful at a time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B less
.RB [ \-EFIMmNRSsX~ ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B less
shows text on the console one screenful at a time, and lets the user
scroll through it and search it.
It shows each
.IR file ,
or standard input when there are no
.I file
operands.
This
.B less
is an applet of
.BR busybox ,
with far fewer options and commands than GNU
.BR less ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
The keyboard is read from the console, not from standard input, so
standard input may be a pipe or a file.
A
.I file
is opened when it is first shown, so a bad name later in the list is
found only when it is reached.
.PP
.B less
pages only when standard output is a console and the console window is
at least 20 columns wide and 3 rows high.
Otherwise it acts as
.BR cat :
it copies each
.IR file ,
or standard input, to standard output unchanged, ignores all options,
and treats a
.I file
named
.B \-
as standard input.
A file that cannot be opened or copied is reported on standard error,
and the remaining files are still copied.
.PP
When standard output is a console and there are no
.I file
operands, standard input must not be a console.
Otherwise there is nothing to show, and
.B less
prints a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.SS Display
The window is the console window when
.B less
starts; see
.BR COLUMNS " and " LINES .
The last row is the prompt, and the rows above it show text.
A line wider than the window wraps onto further rows, unless
.B \-S
is given.
Input that ends with a newline shows an empty row after its last line.
Rows after the end of the input show a
.BR ~ ,
unless
.B \-~
is given.
Scrolling forward stops once one such row shows.
.PP
The prompt is in reverse video, except for a lone colon.
While the first line of the input is at the top, it is the name of the
file, or
.BR "standard input" .
Otherwise it is a
.B :
while more text follows, and
.B (END)
once the end of the input is displayed.
With several files, the name and
.B (END)
are followed by
.BI "(file " i " of " n ) .
The prompt also shows messages, such as
.BR "No matches found" .
.PP
With
.B \-M
or
.B \-m
the prompt is longer, as in
.BR "main.c lines 1-23/100 23%" .
After the file name come
.BI "(file " i " of " n )
when there are several files, the numbers of the first and last lines
shown, and a slash and the total number of lines when that is known.
The total is known for a
.I file
operand that is a regular file, and for other input once all of it has
been read.
The prompt ends with
.B (END)
when the end of the input is displayed, followed by
.B \- next:
and the next file's name when another file follows.
Otherwise it ends with the last line shown as a percentage of the total,
when the total is known.
.B =
and
.B :f
show this prompt whatever the options.
.PP
The input is read as it is needed, enough to fill the window, and more
as commands scroll or search further.
Only the first 9999999 rows are kept, and input beyond them is ignored.
Bytes are shown as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
A tab advances to the next multiple of 8 columns.
.IP \(bu 2
A CR is discarded, so CRLF line endings show as plain line ends, but it
still takes a column when a line is wrapped.
.IP \(bu 2
A backspace removes the character before it, so text overstruck for
bold or underline, as in some formatted pages, shows as its last
character.
After a tab, at the start of a row, or with
.B \-R
after an SGR sequence, it is shown like other control characters.
.IP \(bu 2
Any other control character is shown in reverse video as the character
0x40 above it: NUL as
.BR @ ,
ESC as
.BR [ ,
and so on.
DEL is shown as
.B ?\&
in the same way.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-R ,
an SGR sequence is passed to the console, and takes no columns.
Any other escape sequence is cut down: its ESC is removed, and for a
CSI sequence, so are the
.B [
and any digits and semicolons after it.
What remains, such as the final letter, is shown as text.
.IP \(bu 2
All other bytes are shown as they are.
Text is UTF-8, and each character takes one column, if the ANSI code
page is UTF-8 and the first of
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.B LANG
that is set is not
.BR C .
Otherwise each byte takes one column.
.SS Commands
A command is one key, with no Enter.
Besides the ASCII keys, only Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, and End have
commands.
Any other key does nothing.
Control characters other than Enter and Backspace are skipped wherever
a key is read.
.PP
A command that prompts for text shows the prompt on the last row.
The text is edited only with Backspace, and cannot be longer than fits
on the row.
Enter ends it, and Enter on an empty line cancels the command.
.TP
.BR j ", " e ", " Enter ", " Down
Scroll forward one line.
.TP
.BR k ", " y ", " Up
Scroll back one line.
.TP
.BR f ", " z ", " Space ", " PgDn
Scroll forward one window, which is all the rows above the prompt.
.TP
.BR b ", " w ", " PgUp
Scroll back one window.
.TP
.B d
Scroll forward half a window.
.TP
.B u
Scroll back half a window.
.TP
.BR g ", " < ", " p ", " % ", " Home
Go to the first line.
.TP
.BR G ", " > ", " End
Go to the end of the input.
This reads all the remaining input first.
.TP
.BI / regex
Search forward for the first line after the first line shown that
matches
.IR regex ,
and scroll to put it at the top, as far as the end of the input allows.
If the input read so far has no such line, more is read until one is
found or the input ends.
If no line after it matches, go to the last line that does.
The prompt gives
.B No matches found
when no line matches.
An empty
.I regex
cancels the command and also clears the previous search.
See
.BR Searching .
.TP
.BI ? regex
Like
.BR / ,
but find the last match at or before the first line shown.
If there is none, go to the first match after it.
.TP
.B n
Go to the match after the current one.
.TP
.B N
Go to the match before the current one.
.TP
.BI m letter
Mark the first line shown.
.I letter
is a letter, A to Z or a to z.
Fourteen marks can be set.
After that, new marks replace the oldest, and some older marks are lost.
Marking a letter again, while its first mark is kept, has no effect.
A mark is a position in the displayed rows, and is not tied to a file.
.TP
.BI \(aq letter
Show the line marked with
.I letter
as the first line.
An unset
.I letter
does nothing.
.TP
.BR { ", " ( ", " [
The first line shown must contain the bracket.
Scroll so that the nearest line at or after it that contains the
matching close bracket is the last line shown.
Only the input read so far is searched, and nesting is not tracked.
.TP
.BR } ", " ) ", " ]
The last line shown must contain the bracket.
Scroll so that the nearest line at or before it that contains the
matching open bracket is the first line shown.
Nesting is not tracked.
.TP
.B :n
Show the next file.
.TP
.B :p
Show the previous file.
.TP
.B :x
Show the first file.
.TP
.B :d
Show the previous file, or the next if the current file is the first,
and remove a file from the list: the current file if it is the first
or second, and otherwise the second file.
It has no effect when there is only one file.
.TP
.BR E ", " :e
Prompt for a file name, and show that file instead.
The list of files from the command line is forgotten, so
.B :n
and
.B :p
find no other file.
A file that cannot be read is refused with
.BR "Cannot read this file" .
.TP
.BR = ", " :f
Show the longer prompt of
.BR \-m .
.TP
.B s
Prompt for a file name, and write the input read so far to it,
replacing the file if it exists.
Each row is written as a line, so a wrapped line is broken where it
wrapped.
With
.BR \-R ,
SGR sequences are kept.
.TP
.BI \- flag
Toggle an option.
.I flag
is one of
.BR M ,
.BR m ,
.BR E ,
.BR S ,
.BR N ,
and
.BR ~ .
Toggling
.B \-N
wraps the text again and clears the search.
Toggling
.B \-~
changes nothing visible, as the rows past the end are chosen at startup.
.TP
.BR r ", " R
Redraw the screen.
.TP
.BR q ", " Q ", " :q
Quit.
.PP
Digits typed before a command form a count, which the prompt shows
after a colon.
A count is from 1 to 9999999, and is used by these commands only.
With any other key, or a count out of range, both the count and the
key are discarded.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR e ", " z ", " d ", " Space ", " Enter ", " Down :
scroll forward that many lines.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR y ", " b ", " w ", " u ", " Up :
scroll back that many lines.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR g ", " < ", " G ", " > :
go to that line, counting lines of input, not wrapped rows.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR p ", " % :
go to that percentage of the file.
.IP \(bu 2
.B n
goes that many matches on from the current match.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR / " and " ?\&
ignore the count.
.SS Searching
A search pattern is a POSIX basic regular expression, with the GNU
operators
.BR \e+ ,
.BR \e? ,
and
.BR \e| .
It is matched against each row of text on its own, so a match cannot
span a wrapped line, and
.B ^
and
.B $
match at the ends of a row.
Case matters unless
.B \-I
is given.
With
.BR \-R ,
SGR sequences are not part of the text matched.
An error in the pattern is shown in the prompt, and the previous search
is already cleared.
.PP
The matches of a search are shown in reverse video until the next
search, or until
.B \-N
is toggled.
.B n
and
.B N
move through the matches of the last search in file order, whatever
the direction of that search.
They move from the last match found, not from the screen, and stop at
the first and last matches.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-EF ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
An unknown option prints a usage message on standard error and exits
with status 1.
That includes
.BR \-h ,
which the usage message itself lists.
As the first argument,
.B \-\-help
prints the usage message on standard output and exits with status 0;
see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B \-E
Quit when the end of the input is displayed.
The whole of
.B less
quits, not only the current file.
The flag can be toggled with the
.B \-
command.
.TP
.B \-F
Quit if all of a file's input fits on one screen, when that file is
shown.
With
.BR \-F ,
.B \-E
has no effect.
.TP
.B \-I
Ignore case in all searches.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-m
Show the longer prompt described under
.BR Display .
The two options are the same, and can be toggled separately.
.TP
.B \-N
Put each line number before its line, in 7 columns followed by a space.
A wrapped line repeats its number on each row, and rows past the end of
the input get blank space.
The text is wrapped 8 columns narrower.
The flag can be toggled.
.TP
.B \-S
Truncate long lines: show only the first window width of each line, and
leave the rest out of reach, as there is no scrolling sideways.
The flag can be toggled.
.TP
.B \-R
Pass SGR sequences, which set colors and text styles, to the console.
They are the sequences of the form ESC, then
.BR [ ,
then digits and semicolons, then
.BR m .
Each takes no columns.
A wrapped line carries its style onto its further rows, and every row
that used a style ends with a reset.
Without
.BR \-R ,
ESC is shown as described under
.BR Display ,
so the sequences appear as text.
By default,
.BR man (1)
runs
.B less \-R
to show its emphasis.
.TP
.B \-~
Leave the rows after the end of the input blank, instead of showing
a
.BR ~ .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-X
Accepted and ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
The older form of
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
How the console handles escape sequences and keys; see
.BR busybox (1).
It decides whether
.B less
reads keys with virtual terminal input; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The window width in columns, instead of the console's.
A value from 2 to 29999 is used.
Any other value means 80.
.TP
.B CONEMUPID
When set, the default
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
is 0; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set decides whether text is UTF-8; see
.BR Display .
It also decides how the console is written; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B LESS
Options for every run, read after the command line and scanned one
character at a time.
.BR F ,
.BR M ,
.BR R ,
and
.B S
turn on
.BR \-F ,
.BR \-M ,
.BR \-R ,
and
.BR \-S .
Every other character, including a dash, is ignored, so
.B FRSXMK
is the same as
.BR \-FRSM .
.IP
.BR man (1)
sets it to
.B R
for its pager when it is unset and the output of
.B man
is a console.
.TP
.B LINES
The window height in rows, instead of the console's.
A value from 2 to 29999 is used.
Any other value means 24.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must come before operands.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success:
.B less
quit, the input was copied, or
.B \-\-help
printed the usage message.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, no
.I file
and a console for standard input, a
.I file
that cannot be opened, or a console that cannot be opened.
A file that cannot be opened ends
.B less
at once, even when it is reached late.
When
.B less
acts as
.BR cat ,
a file that cannot be opened or copied also gives 1, after the others
are copied.
.SH EXAMPLES
Number the lines of a file as it is shown:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ less \-N main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Page compiler output, keeping its colors:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ gcc \-fdiagnostics\-color=always \-c main.c 2>&1 | less \-R
.EE
.RE
.PP
View two files with the longer prompt, and type
.B :n
to move to the second:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ less \-M one.txt two.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make every
.BR less ,
including the one that
.BR man (1)
runs, show colors and truncate long lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ export LESS=RS
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B less
uses the console's alternate screen buffer.
It switches to it when it starts paging, and back when it quits, so the
screen then shows what it showed before
.B less
ran, and nothing that
.B less
displayed remains.
That includes the text shown by
.B \-E
or
.BR \-F ,
which quit as soon as the end of the input is displayed.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened after paging has begun ends
.B less
without switching back, so its error message, and whatever runs next,
appear on the alternate screen.
.PP
The window size is read once, when
.B less
starts, and a resize is not noticed.
.PP
Keys are read through the console API, unless standard input is the
console and it already has virtual terminal input enabled, or
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
forces virtual terminal input.
While
.B less
waits for a key, Ctrl+C is read as a key and ignored.
.PP
Standard input is read with blocking reads.
.B less
shows nothing until it has a screenful of input, or the end of the
input, and a command that needs more input waits for it.
.PP
.B less
does not decompress files.
A file named
.B \-
is an ordinary file name when paging.
.PP
Unlike GNU
.BR less :
.IP \(bu 2
The options are those above, and
.B LESS
sets only four of them.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no help command, no
.B +cmd
option, and no command to run a shell command, edit the file, use tags,
or scroll sideways.
.IP \(bu 2
Searches have no modifiers, the regular expressions are basic ones, and
.B n
and
.B N
ignore the direction of the search.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B \-
command toggles only six options, and has no long names.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR LESSOPEN ,
no
.BR LESSKEY ,
and no way to change the prompt.
.PP
w64devkit patches
.B less
so that
.B \-R
passes SGR sequences through, where busybox removes them, and so that a
UTF-8 character takes one column and byte 0x9B is not shown as a
control character.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR man (1)
